//? Title: Leaky bucket as an admission meter
//? Subtitle: Actual burst decisions, lazy leakage and token-bucket duality
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
This implementation meters arrivals: it admits a fitting amount immediately or
rejects it. It has no queue or delayed release schedule, so it does not pace
accepted traffic into a smooth output stream. We retain the original
burst-and-steady-arrival experiment, inspect actual event transitions and show
how complementary token storage gives the same meter in exact arithmetic.
)

\toc

> The actual state transition

Let level L be stored admitted amount, capacity C>0 and leak rate r>=0. At
nondecreasing event time t, with previous committed time s, compute

$$L^- = \max(0,L-r(t-s)),\qquad L^+=\begin{cases}L^-+a&L^-+a\le C,\\L^-&\text{otherwise}.\end{cases}$$
\alttext(Elapsed drainage reduces the stored level; a fitting arrival is added immediately, while a rejected arrival still commits the drained level and timestamp.)

The meter starts empty at time0, so it allows an immediate burst up to C. There
is no output queue, service-completion clock or pacing guarantee. A rejected
event still advances lazy leakage; unused drainage saturates at zero and is not
banked below zero. Same-time arrivals follow caller order.

> One actual rejection after leakage

Fill a capacity5 meter with amount5 at time0, then propose amount4 at time2 with
rate1. Actual leakage leaves3; the proposal would exceed5, so it is rejected and
the committed level remains3. The diagram uses the actual native transition
rather than a reconstructed event.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/leaky_transition.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/leaky_bucket.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/diagram.hpp>
#include <filesystem>
#include <print>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    auto t = dm::algorithms::leaky_bucket_trace(5, 1, {{0, 5}, {2, 4}});
    auto e = t.events.back();
    std::print("| actual time | actual amount | actual old level | actual leaked level | actual accepted | " "actual committed level |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | --- | ---: |\n");
    for (auto &r : t.events)
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", r.time, r.amount,
                   r.before, r.leaked_level, r.accepted, r.after);
    dm::graphics::Diagram d;
    d.node("old", {0, 0},
           std::format("Actual old level\n{} at time{}", e.before,
                       e.previous_time)).node("leak", {6, 0},
                                              std::format(
                                              "Actual leaked level\n{} at time{}",
                                              e.leaked_level, e.time)).node(
        "new", {12, 0},
        std::format("Actual proposal{}\naccepted{}; level{}", e.amount,
                    e.accepted, e.after)).edge("old", "leak").edge("leak", "new").save_svg("build/leaky_transition.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| actual time | actual amount | actual old level | actual leaked level | actual accepted | actual committed level |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | --- | ---: |
| 0 | 5 | 0 | 0 | true | 5 |
| 2 | 4 | 5 | 3 | false | 3 |
\image(figures/leaky_transition.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Actual rejected arrival still commits elapsed leakage.)
\alttext(Three native event states show old level5, leaked level3 and rejected amount4; this is an immediate admission decision, not a queue schedule.)

> Original burst and steady arrivals

Retain capacity5, leak rate1 unit per second, eight unit arrivals at time0 and
one unit arrival at each time1 through6. The first five are admitted together
immediately; the next three overflow. Each later one-unit arrival exactly
replaces one unit of elapsed drainage. Flat post-event level5 does not mean
arrivals were paced: five accepted arrivals share time0. The figure shows actual
event states, with repeated times retained and event order printed.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/leaky_original.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/leaky_bucket.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/plot.hpp>
#include <filesystem>
#include <print>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    std::vector<dm::algorithms::LeakyBucketArrival> a(8, {0, 1});
    for (int i = 1; i <= 6; ++i)
        a.push_back({double(i), 1});
    auto t = dm::algorithms::leaky_bucket_trace(5, 1, a);
    std::vector<double> x, y;
    std::print("| actual event index | actual time | actual accepted | actual leaked level | actual " "post-event level |\n| ---: | ---: | --- | ---: | ---: |\n");
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < t.events.size(); ++i) {
        auto &e = t.events[i];
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", i, e.time, e.accepted,
                   e.leaked_level, e.after);
        x.push_back(e.time);
        y.push_back(e.after);
    }
    std::print("\nActual accepted events {}; actual rejected events {}.\n",
               t.accepted, t.rejected);
    auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    p.line(x, y, "actual event states", {37, 99, 235}, 2).points(x, y, "",
                                                                 {37, 99, 235},
                                                                 4).abline_h(
        5, {120, 120, 120}, 1).y_limits(-.3, 7).title("Original immediate burst and subsequent unit arrivals").x_label("event time in seconds").y_label("actual post-event stored amount").save_svg("build/leaky_original.svg");
}
```
