> Complementary token storage

Define available tokens T=C−L. Then leakage becomes T^−=min(C,T+r*delta_t), and
admitting amount a subtracts it when T^−>=a. Thus an initially empty leaky meter
and an initially full token bucket make the same decisions in exact arithmetic.
The earlier distinction claiming that only the token bucket permits bursts was
incorrect for this meter implementation. A queued leaky-bucket shaper is a
different system.

This dyadic control uses exactly representable quarter units/times, so it can
check native complementary states without a decimal-rounding ambiguity. The
token meter is the library’s existing implementation, not report-side simulated
arithmetic.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/leaky_duality.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/leaky_bucket.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/token_bucket.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/plot.hpp>
#include <filesystem>
#include <print>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    std::vector<dm::algorithms::LeakyBucketArrival> a{{0, 3}, {0, 3}, {.5, 1},
                                                      {1, 2}, {4, 5}, {4, 0},
                                                      {4, 1}, {4.5, 1}};
    auto t = dm::algorithms::leaky_bucket_trace(5, 2, a);
    dm::algorithms::TokenBucket token(5, 2);
    std::vector<double> x, level, tokens;
    std::print("| actual event | actual leaky accepted | actual token accepted | actual leaky level | " "actual available tokens | actual sum |\n| ---: | --- | --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < a.size(); ++i) {
        bool decision = token.allow(a[i].time, a[i].amount);
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", i, t.events[i].accepted,
                   decision, t.events[i].after, token.stored_tokens(),
                   t.events[i].after + token.stored_tokens());
        x.push_back(i);
        level.push_back(t.events[i].after);
        tokens.push_back(token.stored_tokens());
    }
    auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    p.line(x, level, "actual level", {37, 99, 235}, 2).points(x, level, "",
                                                              {37, 99, 235}, 4).line(x, tokens, "actual tokens", {22, 163, 74}, 2).points(x, tokens, "", {22, 163, 74}, 4).y_limits(-.3, 7).title("Actual complementary meter states, capacity5").x_label("caller event index").y_label("stored amount or available tokens").save_svg("build/leaky_duality.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| actual event | actual leaky accepted | actual token accepted | actual leaky level | actual available tokens | actual sum |
| ---: | --- | --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 0 | true | true | 3 | 2 | 5 |
| 1 | false | false | 3 | 2 | 5 |
| 2 | true | true | 3 | 2 | 5 |
| 3 | true | true | 4 | 1 | 5 |
| 4 | true | true | 5 | 0 | 5 |
| 5 | true | true | 5 | 0 | 5 |
| 6 | false | false | 5 | 0 | 5 |
| 7 | true | true | 5 | 0 | 5 |
\image(figures/leaky_duality.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Actual leaky-meter and token-meter decisions on the same dyadic events.)
\alttext(The real native states add to capacity5 and decisions agree; this is an exact-arithmetic duality illustrated without pretending to model a queued shaper.)

> Burst envelope, not a release timetable

Over an interval of length delta_t, at most C units of initial headroom plus
r*delta_t units of new drainage can be used. In exact arithmetic, admitted
amount is bounded by C+r*delta_t. Idle periods can waste drainage, so the bound
need not be attained. This is a volume envelope, not an assertion that
instantaneous output rate is bounded by r. A saturated unit workload at
quarter-second times illustrates real admissions and the time-zero burst. The
continuous line is explicitly a theoretical envelope; points are cumulative sums
of actual accepted amounts. Floating-point arithmetic can introduce small
discrepancies at thresholds.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/leaky_envelope.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/leaky_bucket.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/plot.hpp>
#include <filesystem>
#include <print>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    std::vector<dm::algorithms::LeakyBucketArrival> a;
    for (int k = 0; k <= 40; ++k)
        for (int j = 0; j < 8; ++j)
            a.push_back({k * .25, 1});
    auto t = dm::algorithms::leaky_bucket_trace(5, 1, a);
    std::vector<double> x, y, bound;
    double admitted = 0;
    std::print("| actual event time | cumulative actual admitted amount | theoretical capacity plus rate " "times time |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < t.events.size(); ++i) {
        auto &e = t.events[i];
        if (e.accepted)
            admitted += e.amount;
        if (i + 1 == t.events.size() || t.events[i + 1].time != e.time) {
            x.push_back(e.time);
            y.push_back(admitted);
            bound.push_back(5 + e.time);
            if (int(e.time * 4) % 8 == 0)
                std::print("| {} | {} | {} |\n", e.time, admitted, 5 + e.time);
        }
    }
    auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    p.line(x, bound, "theoretical envelope", {22, 163, 74}, 1.5).points(
        x, y, "actual admitted amount", {37, 99, 235}, 4).y_limits(0, 20).title(
        "Actual admitted volume and explicit burst envelope").x_label(
        "caller time in seconds").y_label("cumulative admitted amount").save_svg("build/leaky_envelope.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| actual event time | cumulative actual admitted amount | theoretical capacity plus rate times time |
| ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 0 | 5 | 5 |
| 2 | 7 | 7 |
| 4 | 9 | 9 |
| 6 | 11 | 11 |
| 8 | 13 | 13 |
| 10 | 15 | 15 |
\image(figures/leaky_envelope.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Actual admitted volumes under a saturated explicit workload.)
\alttext(The theoretical C+r*t line is distinguished from real cumulative acceptance points; simultaneous initial admissions demonstrate the permitted burst.)

> Contracts and verification

Capacity is positive finite and rate nonnegative finite, including zero. Event
times are finite, nonnegative and nondecreasing; amounts finite and nonnegative,
including zero. Invalid single operations throw before changing stored
time/level. Oversized finite amounts are rejected rather than queued; valid
rejection still advances leakage. Ordinary finite arithmetic order is retained;
overflowing elapsed drainage uses a widened fallback and saturates at zero. The
trace validates every event and complete record quota before simulation, then
captures each actual transition once. Its default million-entry cap counts event
records, excludes fields/capacities/bytes, and zero disables only that optional
cap.

Independent exact Fraction transitions verified2000 actual meter events.
Another10000 ordinary events matched the frozen decisions and levels exactly.
Native tests compare24000 dyadic events with complementary native token storage
and retain original burst/steady decisions, rejection leakage, idle/zero
controls, atomic guard failures, complete quotas/ownership and overflowing
drainage. The complete ten-report batch passed77 native CTest cases,39 directly
instrumented ASan/UBSan cases,106 compiled/executed public examples,479/479
scoped definitions and ten representative clangd hovers. All4 complete report
programs executed and all4 native figures were visually reviewed. HTML/Markdown
computed result and identical source/build/export figure bytes passed
verification. Repeatable receipts are retained under
`build/mepml-conversion/batch-194-203/`.

> References

J. Heinanen and R. Guerin(1999),
[RFC2697: A Single Rate Three Color Marker|https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc2697].
Its distinction between a traffic meter and marker, and initially full token
buckets, provides related context; this single-bucket lazy meter does not
implement the RFC’s complete two-bucket three-color algorithm.
